---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Polymer — The Verifiable RPC Network"
  description="Every blockchain app trusts a server it can't audit. Polymer fixes that — every response is signed inside tamper-proof hardware and provably correct before your app acts on it."
  currentPage="protocol"
>

<!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
<section class="hero">
  <canvas id="chrome-bg"></canvas>
  <div class="eyebrow">
    <span>POLYMER <span class="v">/ The Verifiable RPC Network</span></span>
  </div>

  <h1 class="htitle">
    <span class="silver">Verifying the</span><br/>
    <span class="blue">Onchain Economy.</span>
  </h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Every wallet, bridge, and exchange relies on a server it doesn't own to
    read the blockchain. That server could return anything — and there's no
    way to tell. Polymer makes every response <strong>provably correct</strong>
    by signing it inside hardware the operator can't touch.
  </p>

  <div class="cta">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#">Read the whitepaper</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ THE PROBLEM ============ -->
<section class="section" id="problem">
  <div class="marker">01 // THE PROBLEM</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    Your app doesn't read the blockchain. <span class="dim">A server does.</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    Every wallet, bridge, and exchange gets blockchain data through a server
    someone else controls. Encrypting the connection doesn't verify the answer —
    it just means no one intercepted it. If the server itself lies, the lie is
    indistinguishable from the truth.
  </p>

  <!-- Trust gap flow -->
  <div class="trust-gap">
    <div class="tg-node">
      <div class="tg-box">
        <div class="tg-tag">YOUR APP</div>
        <div class="tg-name">wallet · bridge · exchange</div>
        <div class="tg-detail">asks for on-chain data</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tg-conn">
      <div class="tg-conn-track">
        <div class="tg-conn-pulse"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tg-conn-cap">trusts whatever it receives</div>
    </div>

    <div class="tg-node tg-node-warn">
      <div class="tg-warn-ring"></div>
      <div class="tg-box tg-box-warn">
        <div class="tg-tag tg-tag-warn">UNVERIFIABLE SERVER</div>
        <div class="tg-name">someone else's machine</div>
        <div class="tg-detail">could return anything</div>
        <div class="tg-warn-lbl">! no verification possible</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tg-conn">
      <div class="tg-conn-track">
        <div class="tg-conn-pulse"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tg-conn-cap">reads from</div>
    </div>

    <div class="tg-node">
      <div class="tg-box">
        <div class="tg-tag">THE BLOCKCHAIN</div>
        <div class="tg-name">the actual truth</div>
        <div class="tg-detail">immutable · public</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="problem-bullets">
    <div class="pb-item">
      <span class="pb-dash">—</span>
      <span>Encrypting the connection doesn't verify the answer. You know it wasn't intercepted — not that it's correct.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pb-item">
      <span class="pb-dash">—</span>
      <span>A server that lies returns data that looks identical to correct data. No flag. No alert.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pb-item">
      <span class="pb-dash">—</span>
      <span>Every app on chain is exposed to this. It's not theoretical.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Incident -->
  <div class="exploit">
    <div class="exploit-head">
      <span class="sev">CRITICAL</span>
      <span class="id">kelp.layerzero.node-swap</span>
      <span class="ts">2026-04-18T11:14:23Z</span>
      <span class="attr">attribution: Lazarus Group (DPRK)</span>
    </div>

    <div class="exploit-grid">
      <div class="exploit-vis">
        <div class="caption">// What happened · April 18, 2026</div>

        <div class="swap">
          <div class="swap-node honest">
            <div class="hd"><span>HONEST NODE</span><span class="dot"></span></div>
            <div class="label">canonical RPC node</div>
            <div class="hash">serving correct data</div>
            <div class="delta"><span class="k">status:</span> <span class="v">DDoS'd offline</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="swap-arrow">
            <span class="glyph">→</span>
            <span class="lbl">REPLACED BY</span>
          </div>
          <div class="swap-node swapped">
            <div class="hd"><span>POISONED NODE</span><span class="dot"></span></div>
            <div class="label">swapped by attacker</div>
            <div class="hash">same address · different software</div>
            <div class="delta"><span class="k">behavior:</span> <span class="v">selective lie</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="selective">
          <div class="row honest">
            <div class="who">// TO EVERYONE ELSE</div>
            <div class="resp">returned correct data — no alerts triggered</div>
          </div>
          <div class="row lied">
            <div class="who">// TO LAYERZERO'S VERIFIER</div>
            <div class="resp">returned forged data — bridge accepted it</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="resolution-box">
          <div class="rb-label">// WITH POLYMER — THIS ATTACK FAILS</div>
          <div class="rb-body">A swapped binary produces a different hardware fingerprint. The public registry rejects the node. It can't produce a valid signature. The attack has nowhere to go.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exploit-loss">
        <div>
          <span class="single">Drained in a single exploit</span>
          <div class="num"><small>$</small>292<small>M</small></div>
          <div class="num-lbl">Bridge withdrawal · LayerZero rsETH</div>
          <div class="scoped"><strong>116,500 rsETH</strong> · ~18% of circulating supply.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline">
          <div class="tl-lbl">// Attack window</div>
          <div class="tl-rail">
            <div class="seg" style="left:18%; width:64%;"></div>
            <div class="tl-marks">
              <div class="m" style="left:0%;"><span class="cap">T−5m · DDoS honest nodes</span></div>
              <div class="m hot" style="left:18%;"><span class="cap">T+0 · swap binary</span></div>
              <div class="m hot" style="left:50%;"><span class="cap">T+19 · forge answer</span></div>
              <div class="m hot" style="left:82%;"><span class="cap">T+47 · drain bridge</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-bottom">
            <span>UTC 2026-04-18 11:14</span>
            <span class="duration">47 min · 0 alerts</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ THE SOLUTION ============ -->
<section class="section" id="solution">
  <div class="marker">02 // THE SOLUTION</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    Polymer seals the server inside <span class="blue">tamper-proof hardware.</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    The problem isn't that a server reads the blockchain — it's that you can't verify what it's doing.
    Polymer locks the node inside a hardware enclave: a sealed environment where the software is fixed,
    the signing key never leaves, and every answer carries proof it came from the right place.
  </p>

  <div class="compare">
    <div class="compare-col compare-before">
      <div class="cmp-header">
        <span class="cmp-dot cmp-dot-bad"></span>
        <span class="cmp-label">Without Polymer</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cmp-steps">
        <div class="cmp-step">Your app requests data</div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step cmp-bad">Server runs whatever software<br/><span class="cmp-note">operator controls it — can return anything</span></div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step">Answer arrives</div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step cmp-fail">No way to verify it's true</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="compare-vs">
      <div class="compare-vs-line"></div>
      <div class="compare-vs-label">vs</div>
      <div class="compare-vs-line"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="compare-col compare-after">
      <div class="cmp-header">
        <span class="cmp-dot cmp-dot-good"></span>
        <span class="cmp-label cmp-label-good">With Polymer</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cmp-steps">
        <div class="cmp-step">Your app requests data</div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow cmp-arrow-blue">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step cmp-sealed">
          <div class="cmp-sealed-badge">HARDWARE ENCLAVE</div>
          Node runs inside sealed hardware<br/><span class="cmp-note">software is locked — operator can't change it</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow cmp-arrow-blue">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step">Signed answer is returned<br/><span class="cmp-note">signed with a key that never left the hardware</span></div>
        <div class="cmp-arrow cmp-arrow-blue">↓</div>
        <div class="cmp-step cmp-ok">
          <span class="cmp-check">✓</span> Signature verified — answer trusted
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="solution-note">
    The operator can slow or stop the server. They cannot change what it says.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ HOW IT WORKS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="howworks">
  <div class="marker">03 // HOW IT WORKS</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    A request goes in. <span class="blue">A verified answer comes out.</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    Here's what happens between your app sending a request and receiving a response it can trust.
  </p>

  <div class="steps">

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-left">
        <div class="step-num">01</div>
        <div class="step-vline"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-content">
        <div class="step-meta">CALLER</div>
        <div class="step-title">Your app asks for data</div>
        <div class="step-body">Your wallet, bridge, or exchange sends a normal request — a balance, a transaction, an on-chain event. Nothing changes on the caller's side.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-left">
        <div class="step-num">02</div>
        <div class="step-vline"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-content">
        <div class="step-meta">HARDWARE ENCLAVE</div>
        <div class="step-title">A sealed node reads the blockchain</div>
        <div class="step-body">Polymer's node fetches the data from inside a hardware enclave — a sealed environment that Intel's chip controls. The operator chose which software to run inside it. Once it's running, they can't change it or see inside.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-left">
        <div class="step-num">03</div>
        <div class="step-vline"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-content">
        <div class="step-meta">SEALED SIGNATURE</div>
        <div class="step-title">The node signs the answer inside the hardware</div>
        <div class="step-body">The enclave generated a private signing key when it booted — inside the hardware, never written to disk. The operator can't access it. The node uses it to sign every response. A forged answer would need that key. Nobody has it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step step-last">
      <div class="step-left">
        <div class="step-num step-num-final">04</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-content">
        <div class="step-meta">ON-CHAIN VERIFICATION</div>
        <div class="step-title">Your app verifies before acting</div>
        <div class="step-body">Your app checks the signature against a public key registered on-chain. If the signature is valid, the data came from a Polymer node running the correct software. If not, discard it. No trust in the operator required.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ TRUST PILLARS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="pillars">
  <div class="marker">04 // WHY YOU CAN TRUST IT</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    Three independent checks. <span class="blue">Hardware.</span> Signatures. <span class="dim">Open source.</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    Each pillar works independently. Compromise one and the other two still hold.
    No single point of trust.
  </p>

  <div class="pillars">
    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">01</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">HARDWARE</div>
      <div class="pillar-title">The hardware seals it shut</div>
      <div class="pillar-analogy">Like tamper-evident packaging — you can tell if it's been opened.</div>
      <div class="pillar-body">Intel's chip creates a sealed environment and signs a fingerprint of what software is running inside it. Not the host machine, not the cloud provider, not the operator can alter what's inside. The chip itself is the witness.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">02</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">SIGNATURES</div>
      <div class="pillar-title">Every answer is signed</div>
      <div class="pillar-analogy">Like a notarized document — the signature proves it came from the right source.</div>
      <div class="pillar-body">The node generates a signing key inside the enclave at boot. It never leaves the hardware. Every response is signed with it. You can verify the signature against a public key on-chain. The operator never had access to the private key — they can't forge it.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">03</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">OPEN SOURCE</div>
      <div class="pillar-title">The code is public</div>
      <div class="pillar-analogy">Like an open recipe — anyone can check that what's being served matches what's written.</div>
      <div class="pillar-body">Polymer's node software is public and built deterministically. Anyone can take the source code, compile it themselves, and confirm the result matches what's running on the network. No hidden modifications are possible.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ INVESTORS ============ -->
<section class="section investors-section" id="investors">
  <div class="marker">05 // BACKED BY</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    Backed by <span class="blue">long-term capital</span> across crypto.
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    Polymer is funded by investors who've been backing on-chain
    infrastructure since the early cycles.
  </p>

  <div class="investor-grid">
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/distributedglobal.webp" alt="Distributed Global" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/blockchaincapital.webp" alt="Blockchain Capital" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/maven.webp" alt="Maven 11" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/coinbase.webp" alt="Coinbase Ventures" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/digitalcurrencygroup.webp" alt="Digital Currency Group" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/placeholder.webp" alt="Placeholder" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/figmentcapital.webp" alt="Figment Capital" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/informalsystems.webp" alt="Informal Systems" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/cosmostation.webp" alt="Cosmostation" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/wintermute.webp" alt="Wintermute" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/longhash.webp" alt="LongHash Ventures" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/niv.webp" alt="NIV" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/tauren.webp" alt="Tauren" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/thelao.webp" alt="The LAO" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/basisset.webp" alt="Basis Set Ventures" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/signum.webp" alt="Signum" loading="lazy"/></div>
    <div class="investor"><img src="/assets/investors/uob.webp" alt="UOB Venture" loading="lazy"/></div>
